I am experimenting with using multiple instances of R in a single java
application. I know I cannot run more than one at the same time, but I
assumed I should be able to stop and restart R engine consecutively. It
doesn't seem to be working though.
Here is a simple piece of code (directly adopted from the examples) I am
using for testing:

    // ...
    System.out.println("Creating Rengine ...");
    Rengine re=new Rengine(null, false, null);
    System.out.println("Rengine created, waiting for R");
    if (!re.waitForR()) {
            System.out.println("Cannot load R");
            return;
    }
    try {
        re.eval("a <- sqrt(36)");
        re.eval("b <- log(100)");
        System.out.println ("a = " + re.eval("a"));
        System.out.println("b = " + re.eval("b"));
        System.out.println("a + b = " + re.eval("a + b"));
    } catch (Exception e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
    re.end();

    // ... and repeat the whole thing again:

    System.out.println("Creating Rengine ...");
    re=new Rengine(null, false, null);
    System.out.println("Rengine created, waiting for R");
    if (!re.waitForR()) {
            System.out.println("Cannot load R");
            return;
    }
    try {
        re.eval("a <- sqrt(36)");
        re.eval("b <- log(100)");
        System.out.println("a = " + re.eval("a"));
        System.out.println("b = " + re.eval("b"));
        System.out.println("a + b = " + re.eval("a + b"));
    } catch (Exception e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
    re.end();

The second time around my application exits silently when Rengine
constructor is called. What actually happens, and how can I make it work?

Thanks,
Anya

